 I didn't want to  become a lawyer because you know it's  boring to be a lawyer in Latin America  and I stumble upon  ufm without knowing that it was like a  libertarian safe-haven maybe if I would  have gone to the University Francisco  marroquín and Guatemala City yes but I  didn't know what classical liberalism  was I just chose that University because  it offered the the career that I wanted  maybe if I would have gotten to you know  a normal state university I would be  Marxist today and there I was confronted  with the free market economics which at  first I was very acceptable off but once  I understood philosophically the  importance of Liberty and you know  regarding individual rights as the core  of society I said okay this this can  work but I still was accepting all that  Guatemala was ready for them and then I  went and getting scholarships I studied  in the u.s. in Europe I was confronted  with the other side of ideas like  socialist Marxist ideas in Belgium in  Italy and I did some work with African  immigrants in Europe and I start seeing  the common line between people wanting  to have a better life so that's where I  got convinced that definitely Liberty  works and when I went back to what Amala  I started being very outspoken about  this on the radio and I started to  become you know  renown for that one of the things that  is striking I'm both in the libertarian  world in general but especially in a  kind of Latin American context is you're  outspoken about social issues as well as  economic issues because a lot of Latin  American libertarians will talk about  what we need free markets but then they  tend to be conservative in social issues  or they just don't talk about it  why why are social issues and cultural  issues so important to you because when  I went back on air in Guatemala and I  started talking with young people I  noticed that the lack of self esteem and  believing in yourself and emotional  intelligence and being resilient was the  key of why young people rejected free  markets you cannot convince people of  free economies if at first they feel  afraid of being free so I started  noticing that psychologically we have a  culture that it embraces you to be a  victim to find in others the guilt of  why you are poor or why you're not  achieving whatever you want to achieve  there's also a component of not  disturbing Society of not speaking up if  you speak if you think differently so  that's why for me social issues are so  important  you're a great equal opportunity  offender you haven't you talked at  conservatives as well as ads that  left-wingers what is different how do  you approach a conservative to convince  them that libertarian ideas make sense  and how do you talk to a socialist  so with the Socialists it's all about  the Socratic method instead of me  telling them why am i right I try to  question them on why their project is  good and once I do that I force them to  go back into the logical stream of  whatever they're proposing so for  example they say let's take away from  the rich to give to the poor okay but  how does that work  why would you take what is the list of  goods that you would make the government  give versus the ones that would be  private and that's where they get like  caught up with the Conservatives  you have to convince them of is that  free-market economics is not enough and  that there are there are things that  they defend out of dogma and what is  happening in Latin America is that the  left-wing is grabbing some flags that  appeal to young people like the gay flag  the environment flag the feminist flag  and conservatives think that just by  ignoring these subjects they will  disappear and they are not we're just  losing young people to the socialist  side what how as a libertarian how do  you engage things like gay rights the  environment and feminism just to mention  the things that you brought up well  first of all for the environment I I am  a strong supporter and I have get  involved in a market solutions for  pollution so I believe that it's not  only like I don't know about the climate  change because I'm not an expert but it  is evident that we're polluting the  planet so I for example started a  reforestation movement in 2012 that it  became self sustainable selling  forestation plans to different companies  so they could get like you know it tax  exemptions or something like that I  promote ideas that come from the market  to you know better the environment I I  never talk about like the government to  be the solution what I notice is that  the Conservatives don't use these market  solutions for pollution in their speech  but when you appeal to young people with  them they get interested with feminism I  go back to individual rights regardless  of your gender you have three individual  rights life liberty and private property  which are coincidentally their three  rights that are not guaranteed in Latin  America so my approach is if we will  have these three rights covered any  woman or man or gay man or whatever  could overcome the lack of opportunity  what are what are the worst places in  Latin America for kind of individual  freedom and liberty and what are the  best places I think that all the  countries in Latin America have a lakota  me [ __ ]  in the capital cities so if you go to  when Osiris or Madrid or Sao Paulo or  Mexico City or even Guatemala City  you're gonna see more respect for women  for the indigenous people for gay people  but if you go to the rural areas  you're still gonna encounter a very  machista backwards mentality where you  know women rights are not respected or  where there is racism and classism still  are there are there bright spots I mean  beyond the cities are there particular  countries or governments that are doing  good things or or are there I don't know  groups of people who are really kind of  making a difference I think it's um it's  not all black and white because what  really intrigues me is that the richest  countries of Latin America once they hit  some economic development like the case  of Cuba which was the richest country of  Latin America but not only that  culturally it was more open for women  for black people even for homosexual  communities and then they had the Castro  regime and everything was ruined then  you have Chile or even Venezuela  so what intrigues me is why the richest  countries of Latin America once they hit  this development they go backwards in  individual freedom  whereas poor countries like mine have  safe havens like UFM or leave a top  police radio it seems to me that when  you don't reach that level of wealth  people appreciate more individual  liberty what do you think explains that  dynamic of hitting a certain level of  wealth or comfort and then kind of going  socialist why why is that we have a  saying in Spanish it's like a poor  grandfather rich father miserable  grandson so what this explains is that  in three generations there's one person  that works hard to get out of poverty  the son of that of that father notices  how hard it was to work that but then  the grandson had everything given so he  doesn't remember like the hard times so  you have this  click Oh a neverending story yeah so  that's like Joseph Schumpeter talks  about that in his work you were gonna  run for president of Guatemala but  you're not old enough yet so good for  you because whether you have to be 40  right now you're under 40 good for you  let's pretend that you've ran and won  what would you be doing differently than  what's going on here first I would do a  reduction of the executive branch  instead of 14 ministries and 15  secretaries I would reduce it to four  making together things that I think  should work together like for example  only one ministry of resources with the  whole purpose of putting this back in  individual hands not the government  taking care of it and then I would do  tax decentralization so that each state  of Guatemala would be autonomous and  also 50 percent of the national budget I  would dedicate to the line of security  and justice because precisely what I was  telling you life liberty and private  property are the three rights that are  not respected in Guatemala so we need  strong judicial systems that take care  of the crimes once they are committed  until the final punishment is completed  and flat tax I think that if you reduce  a government to basic functions we'd  only want tax that it can be I don't  know the sales tax you can run the  machine what is the word what's the  worst part of the current Guatemalan  regime centralization in the capital  city I think the fact that there is some  mentality that the rest of the country  is so poor that they depend on the  capital so everywhere everything comes  here and that's why big corruption is  isn't closed here and this way you  cannot have individual development Latin  America similar to the United States had  has a youth boom a lot of young people  and a lot of old people and the old  people are growing more and more  dependent on the state for pensions and  things like that what are the big  you know what are the big points of  friction between the young and the old  in Latin America and how how is that  gonna play out one of the main  complaints that I get from other people  is how come young people don't remember  the eeper inflation dates of Mexico how  come people don't remember the parinita  era they complain that young people  became socialists but I also noticed  something that is that the old people  never took the time to sit down and  explain history and and you know by  storytelling to their sons and grandsons  in my case my Cuban grandparents made it  very clear for me you know like with  stories and because I also had family  that became communist back in Cuba so if  you don't talk about these issues young  people are never never gonna know about  them and for young people the main  complaint to the old is that they don't  modernize they say okay they might tell  me about free markets but they don't  want to talk about legalizing drugs and  homosexuality and you know like a more  globalized world they are stuck with  their traditions so I think that's the  main friction what are some other ways  to bring libertarian ideas to young  people in Latin America I mean part of  it is that the storytelling really  remembering the past and kind of talking  about that what are what are other  methods I think that we have to talk  about successful stories of that like I  am a true believer of what Hayek said  that if if you have elites with the  proper right ideas they will inspire the  rest of society I don't believe that  majorities change things it's always  minorities well-organized and with good  ideas so if we want to influence young  people we we have to know who are the  singers the songwriters the football  players the telenovela actors and  actresses that they follow and try to  persuade those leaders into having this  ideas which some of them are getting  like I'm pretty happy when like for  example Brad Pitt or Ricky Martin or  Antonio Banderas go on and complain  about the Venezuelan regime or how  capitalism like bono from u2  after decades of giving money to Africa  so they finally get it if they're  intellectually honest so I think we  should appeal to young people by their  hobbies or interests what what they like  you know the United's the United States  and Guatemala have a difficult history  of in the United States abetted horrible  things happening in Guatemala and that's  in many ways that's true of Latin  America more broadly what should the  relationship be between the United  States and Guatemala and Latin America  more broadly well first of all I think  that we have to own our [ __ ] I can't say  because no one comes inside your house  unless you let them so let me tell you  one example in what the money in the 50s  there was I don't know if you say it in  English inoculation of diseases in our  psychiatric hospitals in our jails  purposefully doctors from the United  States wanted to experiment with  syphilis and other diseases in Guatemala  so when the CIA opened this documents  all the left-wing of what the malla was  like oh my god this happened how  horrible until there were a document  signed by the left-wing  FDR of our era Juan Jose arrival  proving that the president who was a  socialist gave permission to the United  States to do this so first of all I  think that Latin America has to  understand that every time that the US  has get involved is because either the  government or the opposition or someone  inside the country said yes please come  in that's the first thing the second is  we need to have a better conversation on  how to open markets that would stop the  immigration to the US if people want to  live our countries we need to have  something that appeals for them to stay  so for instance if we legalize drugs a  new market of marijuana medicinal  marijuana tourism can start in Guatemala  so that people don't want to leave  we need to sell it that way and the  third I think it's foreign aid because a  lot of Americans with their best  intentions give money to some NGOs  without knowing that this NGO sometimes  financed the conflicts that make it  impossible for people to stay and have a  job is there the u.s. government beyond  the foreign aid question and should they  generally be not getting involved in in  Latin American politics or should it all  be through trade and culture as opposed  to anything else I think that the least  government interventions we have the  better what we need is more individual  contact for me for example is more  useful that for example in this  conference  I was wearing this what a modern typical  shawl and some women were like oh my god  where did you get that and I'm like oh  do you want to go to the lady that makes  it for me 